1
00:00:00,335 --> 00:00:01,723
When you put a thousand dollars in

2
00:00:01,723 --> 00:00:02,385
the bank,

3
00:00:02,385 --> 00:00:03,485
where does it go?

4
00:00:04,285 --> 00:00:05,885
Most people think the bank keeps a

5
00:00:05,885 --> 00:00:06,660
little of it,

6
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:08,585
and lends the rest to somebody else

7
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:10,560
That's what I was taught.

8
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:12,410
It's probably what you were taught too

9
00:00:13,135 --> 00:00:14,585
It's not how it works.

10
00:00:15,427 --> 00:00:17,077
When a bank gives someone a loan

11
00:00:17,177 --> 00:00:19,577
it doesn't hand over anybody's savings.

12
00:00:19,577 --> 00:00:22,002
It types brand new money into existence

13
00:00:22,927 --> 00:00:24,827
That's not a conspiracy theory.

14
00:00:24,827 --> 00:00:26,227
It's what the Bank of England,

15
00:00:26,227 --> 00:00:27,664
Britain's central bank,

16
00:00:27,664 --> 00:00:30,227
wrote in plain English in twenty fourteen

17
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:32,210
And once you see it,

18
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:34,510
you'll understand why booms get so big

19
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:36,560
why crashes hit so hard,

20
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:37,698
and what the number in your bank

21
00:00:37,698 --> 00:00:39,185
account really is.

22
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:46,610
Here's the version in most textbooks.

23
00:00:47,218 --> 00:00:49,355
Lena saves a thousand dollars and puts

24
00:00:49,355 --> 00:00:50,368
it in the bank.

25
00:00:51,035 --> 00:00:52,472
The bank keeps a slice,

26
00:00:52,472 --> 00:00:53,685
say ten percent,

27
00:00:53,685 --> 00:00:54,698
a hundred dollars,

28
00:00:54,698 --> 00:00:55,735
in reserve.

29
00:00:55,735 --> 00:00:57,260
And it lends the other nine hundred

30
00:00:57,260 --> 00:00:58,185
to Sam.

31
00:00:58,852 --> 00:00:59,852
Sam spends it.

32
00:00:59,852 --> 00:01:01,352
The shop puts it in its bank

33
00:01:01,465 --> 00:01:02,965
That bank keeps ten percent,

34
00:01:02,965 --> 00:01:04,252
and lends out the rest.

35
00:01:04,252 --> 00:01:05,452
And on it goes.

36
00:01:06,102 --> 00:01:08,164
It's called the money multiplier.

37
00:01:08,164 --> 00:01:09,027
In this story,

38
00:01:09,027 --> 00:01:10,677
the bank is just a middleman,

39
00:01:10,677 --> 00:01:13,277
passing savers' money on to borrowers.

40
00:01:13,868 --> 00:01:14,718
It's neat.

41
00:01:14,718 --> 00:01:15,843
It's tidy.

42
00:01:15,843 --> 00:01:17,468
And according to the Bank of England

43
00:01:17,518 --> 00:01:19,243
it is not an accurate description of

44
00:01:19,243 --> 00:01:20,818
how money is created.

45
00:01:21,593 --> 00:01:22,343
Their paper,

46
00:01:22,343 --> 00:01:24,693
called Money Creation in the Modern Economy

47
00:01:24,793 --> 00:01:26,018
puts it like this.

48
00:01:26,718 --> 00:01:29,331
Rather than banks receiving deposits when households

49
00:01:29,331 --> 00:01:31,356
save and then lending them out,

50
00:01:31,356 --> 00:01:33,493
bank lending creates deposits.

51
00:01:34,285 --> 00:01:35,260
Read that again.

52
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,635
The loan comes first.

53
00:01:36,635 --> 00:01:38,710
The deposit is created by the loan

54
00:01:41,193 --> 00:01:42,543
Let's run it properly.

55
00:01:42,543 --> 00:01:44,568
Sam wants to borrow ten thousand dollars

56
00:01:44,618 --> 00:01:45,593
for a car.

57
00:01:46,202 --> 00:01:47,840
The bank checks he's likely to pay

58
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:48,577
it back.

59
00:01:48,577 --> 00:01:50,777
Then it does something remarkably simple.

60
00:01:51,468 --> 00:01:53,381
It adds ten thousand dollars to Sam's

61
00:01:53,381 --> 00:01:54,193
account.

62
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:55,560
That's it.

63
00:01:55,510 --> 00:01:57,272
Nobody's savings went down.

64
00:01:57,272 --> 00:01:59,535
Lena still has her thousand dollars.

65
00:01:59,535 --> 00:02:01,360
Sam now has ten thousand he didn't

66
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:02,585
have a minute ago.

67
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:04,723
Look at the bank's books.

68
00:02:04,723 --> 00:02:05,823
Two new lines appear,

69
00:02:05,823 --> 00:02:07,060
at the same moment.

70
00:02:07,693 --> 00:02:08,381
On one side,

71
00:02:08,381 --> 00:02:09,168
a loan.

72
00:02:09,168 --> 00:02:11,318
Sam owes the bank ten thousand dollars

73
00:02:11,418 --> 00:02:13,118
That's something the bank owns.

74
00:02:13,735 --> 00:02:14,573
On the other side,

75
00:02:14,573 --> 00:02:15,535
a deposit.

76
00:02:15,535 --> 00:02:17,860
The bank owes Sam ten thousand dollars

77
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:19,660
That's something the bank owes.

78
00:02:20,318 --> 00:02:21,705
Both were created out of nothing,

79
00:02:21,705 --> 00:02:23,093
with a few keystrokes.

80
00:02:23,093 --> 00:02:25,343
And Sam's deposit is real money.

81
00:02:25,343 --> 00:02:26,143
He can spend it,

82
00:02:26,143 --> 00:02:26,918
transfer it,

83
00:02:26,918 --> 00:02:28,668
pay the car dealer with it.

84
00:02:29,485 --> 00:02:31,485
In the Bank of England's own words

85
00:02:31,585 --> 00:02:33,435
whenever a bank makes a loan,

86
00:02:33,435 --> 00:02:36,197
it simultaneously creates a matching deposit in

87
00:02:36,197 --> 00:02:37,760
the borrower's bank account,

88
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:39,635
thereby creating new money.

89
00:02:40,435 --> 00:02:42,085
And it works backwards too.

90
00:02:42,085 --> 00:02:43,610
When Sam pays the loan back,

91
00:02:43,610 --> 00:02:45,260
both lines are crossed out.

92
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:46,735
The money disappears.

93
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,885
Just as taking out a new loan

94
00:02:48,885 --> 00:02:49,935
creates money,

95
00:02:49,935 --> 00:02:52,435
the repayment of bank loans destroys money

96
00:02:54,952 --> 00:02:56,202
So how much of our money is

97
00:02:56,202 --> 00:02:57,239
made this way?

98
00:02:57,239 --> 00:02:59,027
Start with the money you can hold

99
00:02:59,843 --> 00:03:01,218
Notes and coins.

100
00:03:01,218 --> 00:03:02,643
When the Bank of England wrote its

101
00:03:02,643 --> 00:03:03,330
paper,

102
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:04,918
cash was just three percent of the

103
00:03:04,918 --> 00:03:07,043
money in circulation in Britain.

104
00:03:07,635 --> 00:03:09,410
The other ninety-seven percent?

105
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:10,422
Bank deposits.

106
00:03:10,422 --> 00:03:11,935
Numbers on screens.

107
00:03:12,602 --> 00:03:13,589
And those deposits,

108
00:03:13,589 --> 00:03:14,327
it says,

109
00:03:14,327 --> 00:03:17,152
are mostly created by commercial banks themselves

110
00:03:18,093 --> 00:03:19,456
So the number in your account isn't

111
00:03:19,456 --> 00:03:20,831
a pile of cash with your name

112
00:03:20,831 --> 00:03:21,431
on it,

113
00:03:21,418 --> 00:03:22,768
sitting in a vault.

114
00:03:23,368 --> 00:03:24,343
It's a promise.

115
00:03:24,343 --> 00:03:25,868
An I. O. U. from your bank

116
00:03:25,968 --> 00:03:27,793
The bank owes you that money.

117
00:03:28,627 --> 00:03:30,240
And the idea is old.

118
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:31,577
In the sixteen hundreds,

119
00:03:31,577 --> 00:03:34,052
London goldsmiths stored people's gold,

120
00:03:34,052 --> 00:03:36,077
and handed out paper receipts.

121
00:03:36,668 --> 00:03:38,393
People started paying each other with the

122
00:03:38,393 --> 00:03:40,268
receipts instead of the gold.

123
00:03:40,268 --> 00:03:42,380
And the goldsmiths noticed something.

124
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:44,506
Hardly anyone came back for their gold

125
00:03:44,506 --> 00:03:45,843
at the same time.

126
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:48,073
So they lent out receipts for gold

127
00:03:48,073 --> 00:03:49,147
that wasn't there.

128
00:03:49,147 --> 00:03:49,760
Promises,

129
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:50,810
spent as money.

130
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:51,985
That's banking.

131
00:03:54,327 --> 00:03:56,477
Which raises the obvious question.

132
00:03:56,477 --> 00:03:58,627
If banks can create money by typing

133
00:03:58,727 --> 00:04:00,352
why don't they just type more?

134
00:04:01,002 --> 00:04:02,727
The Bank of England lists three main

135
00:04:02,727 --> 00:04:03,502
limits.

136
00:04:04,243 --> 00:04:04,843
One.

137
00:04:04,643 --> 00:04:06,268
The bank has to make a profit

138
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:07,843
Lend to people who can't pay you

139
00:04:07,843 --> 00:04:08,443
back,

140
00:04:08,156 --> 00:04:09,218
and you lose.

141
00:04:09,218 --> 00:04:10,593
In a competitive market,

142
00:04:10,593 --> 00:04:12,818
banks can't lend without limit and stay

143
00:04:12,818 --> 00:04:13,793
profitable.

144
00:04:13,793 --> 00:04:15,731
And regulators make them hold a cushion

145
00:04:15,731 --> 00:04:17,143
of their own capital.

146
00:04:17,835 --> 00:04:18,435
Two.

147
00:04:18,210 --> 00:04:19,322
The rest of us.

148
00:04:19,322 --> 00:04:21,760
Households and companies can destroy money too

149
00:04:21,885 --> 00:04:23,785
just by paying off their debts.

150
00:04:24,552 --> 00:04:25,152
Three,

151
00:04:24,877 --> 00:04:26,152
and most important.

152
00:04:26,152 --> 00:04:27,402
The central bank.

153
00:04:27,402 --> 00:04:28,752
By setting interest rates,

154
00:04:28,752 --> 00:04:31,252
it makes borrowing cheaper or more expensive

155
00:04:31,365 --> 00:04:33,477
That's the brake and the accelerator.

156
00:04:34,068 --> 00:04:36,868
The paper calls monetary policy the ultimate

157
00:04:36,868 --> 00:04:38,768
constraint on money creation.

158
00:04:39,585 --> 00:04:41,910
Now here's the part that surprises people

159
00:04:42,010 --> 00:04:43,710
What about the rule that banks must

160
00:04:43,710 --> 00:04:46,010
keep a slice of deposits in reserve

161
00:04:46,793 --> 00:04:48,368
Britain doesn't have one.

162
00:04:48,368 --> 00:04:49,318
And in America,

163
00:04:49,318 --> 00:04:50,793
since March twenty twenty,

164
00:04:50,793 --> 00:04:53,468
the reserve requirement has been zero percent

165
00:04:54,385 --> 00:04:56,285
Banks aren't limited by how much cash

166
00:04:56,285 --> 00:04:57,135
they keep.

167
00:04:57,135 --> 00:04:59,110
They're limited by whether lending is worth

168
00:04:59,110 --> 00:04:59,710
it.

169
00:05:02,018 --> 00:05:03,368
Now you can see why booms get

170
00:05:03,368 --> 00:05:04,293
so big.

171
00:05:04,835 --> 00:05:05,760
When times are good,

172
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:07,022
banks lend more.

173
00:05:07,022 --> 00:05:09,122
More loans means more deposits.

174
00:05:09,122 --> 00:05:11,172
More money chases the same houses.

175
00:05:11,172 --> 00:05:12,485
Prices rise.

176
00:05:13,127 --> 00:05:15,240
Higher prices mean bigger loans.

177
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,152
Bigger loans create more money.

178
00:05:17,152 --> 00:05:18,802
And the loop feeds itself.

179
00:05:19,593 --> 00:05:21,293
And it runs in reverse.

180
00:05:21,293 --> 00:05:22,993
When people get scared and pay down

181
00:05:22,993 --> 00:05:23,593
debt,

182
00:05:23,255 --> 00:05:24,743
and banks stop lending,

183
00:05:24,743 --> 00:05:26,543
money actually disappears.

184
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:28,723
The Bank of England says the fall

185
00:05:28,723 --> 00:05:30,660
in bank lending in Britain after two

186
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:33,110
thousand eight is an important reason money

187
00:05:33,110 --> 00:05:35,298
grew so much more slowly than before

188
00:05:35,298 --> 00:05:36,260
the crash.

189
00:05:38,568 --> 00:05:40,456
There's one more thing this explains.

190
00:05:40,456 --> 00:05:41,618
Bank runs.

191
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:44,010
Your deposit is a promise.

192
00:05:44,010 --> 00:05:45,410
But the bank doesn't keep all its

193
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:46,997
promises as cash.

194
00:05:46,997 --> 00:05:48,610
Most of what it owns is loans

195
00:05:48,648 --> 00:05:50,685
which won't be paid back for years

196
00:05:51,485 --> 00:05:52,885
Normally that's fine,

197
00:05:52,885 --> 00:05:54,560
because only a few people want their

198
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:56,235
money on any given day.

199
00:05:57,052 --> 00:05:58,990
In September two thousand seven,

200
00:05:58,990 --> 00:06:02,102
savers queued outside Northern Rock in Britain

201
00:06:02,227 --> 00:06:03,990
It was the country's first bank run

202
00:06:03,990 --> 00:06:05,877
in a hundred and fifty years.

203
00:06:06,685 --> 00:06:08,260
In March twenty twenty-three,

204
00:06:08,260 --> 00:06:10,585
customers of Silicon Valley Bank pulled out

205
00:06:10,585 --> 00:06:12,610
more than forty billion dollars in a

206
00:06:12,610 --> 00:06:13,722
single day,

207
00:06:13,722 --> 00:06:15,198
mostly with a few taps on their

208
00:06:15,198 --> 00:06:16,135
phones.

209
00:06:16,852 --> 00:06:19,427
Management expected another hundred billion the next

210
00:06:19,427 --> 00:06:20,139
day.

211
00:06:20,139 --> 00:06:21,014
That day,

212
00:06:21,014 --> 00:06:22,727
regulators shut it down.

213
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,997
That's why governments insure deposits.

214
00:06:25,997 --> 00:06:27,035
In the US,

215
00:06:27,035 --> 00:06:28,735
the F. D. I. C. covers up

216
00:06:28,735 --> 00:06:30,785
to two hundred and fifty thousand dollars

217
00:06:30,785 --> 00:06:31,860
per depositor,

218
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:32,760
at each bank,

219
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:34,360
for each type of account.

220
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:35,818
So,

221
00:06:35,468 --> 00:06:36,768
back to the question.

222
00:06:36,768 --> 00:06:38,181
When you put a thousand dollars in

223
00:06:38,181 --> 00:06:38,781
the bank,

224
00:06:38,618 --> 00:06:39,718
where does it go?

225
00:06:40,368 --> 00:06:41,718
It doesn't go anywhere.

226
00:06:41,718 --> 00:06:43,193
It becomes a promise.

227
00:06:43,193 --> 00:06:44,580
And when someone else borrows,

228
00:06:44,580 --> 00:06:46,393
the bank doesn't hand them your money

229
00:06:47,202 --> 00:06:48,277
It makes new money.

230
00:06:48,277 --> 00:06:49,302
With a keyboard.

231
00:06:49,302 --> 00:06:51,277
The moment someone signs for a loan

232
00:06:52,218 --> 00:06:53,518
Most of the money in the world

233
00:06:53,518 --> 00:06:54,543
wasn't printed.

234
00:06:54,543 --> 00:06:55,693
It was typed.

235
00:06:56,535 --> 00:06:58,135
And if you don't understand that,

236
00:06:58,135 --> 00:06:59,735
you don't understand money.

